Jahshaka patch 0088 - a MIXED shadow-mapping VAO list is legal.

SOURCE patch. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after pulling
this. It touches `OgreMain/src/OgreSubMesh2.cpp`, which NO other patch in the
stack touches, so it cannot overlap; a tree that fails `build-ogre.sh` with
"upstream changed the patched file" here is seeing a different patch's known
overlap false positive (CLAUDE.md): reset the ogre-next submodule and re-run the
whole stack.

THE PIN DEFECT (recorded 2026-09-15 as ATOM-1's finding, dodged on our side until
now; SPECS/OGRE_UPSTREAM_ISSUES.md "A MIXED shadow-VAO list is a double free").
`SubMesh::destroyShadowMappingVaos` decides whether the shadow list ALIASES the
normal list from ONE test, `mVao[VpNormal][0] == mVao[VpShadow][0]`, and applies
the answer to the whole list. So exactly two shapes are legal: every entry an
alias, or every entry independent. A MIXED list reads as fully independent, the
aliased entries are destroyed here, and `~SubMesh` destroys the very same VAOs and
their shared vertex buffer a second time through `mVao[VpNormal]`:
"Vertex Buffer has already been destroyed or doesn't belong to this VaoManager",
thrown on the first mesh destroy after an import.

WHY A MIXED LIST IS THE SHAPE A LOD CHAIN WANTS. The optimized shadow form is a
position-only vertex buffer with duplicate vertices merged: it exists so a shadow
pass streams 12 bytes per vertex instead of 48. Its value is proportional to the
vertex fetch a pass actually does, and a COARSE level of the chain does a fraction
of it (each level halves the triangles), while an independent VAO per level costs
an index buffer and a VertexArrayObject per level per mesh, forever, in VRAM. The
honest shape is therefore ONE optimized VAO for level 0 and the NORMAL VAOs
aliased for the coarse levels - which is what Jahshaka's `buildMeshV2` now builds
(irisgl/engine/src/OgreMesh.cpp), and which the pin refused to let it free.

THE FIX, and why it is the whole fix. Erase from the shadow list every entry that
IS an entry of the normal list, then destroy what is left. For the two pure shapes
this is bit-for-bit the old behaviour (all-aliased erases everything and destroys
nothing; all-independent erases nothing and destroys everything), and for a mixed
list it destroys exactly the entries this SubMesh owns. No new state is stored, so
nothing can fall out of sync with the lists themselves; the lists are one entry
per LOD level, so the linear search is a handful of pointer compares.
`<algorithm>` is already in scope through OgreStdHeaders.h.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0087 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane
worktree. Suites: `meshbake.shadow_vao_alias` (a chained mesh holds ONE optimized
shadow VAO and its coarse levels alias the normal ones) and `test_engine_recreate`
(the suite that caught the double free in the first place) - plus every shadow
pixel suite, since the geometry a coarse shadow level draws is unchanged.

--- a/OgreMain/src/OgreSubMesh2.cpp
+++ b/OgreMain/src/OgreSubMesh2.cpp
@@ -1535,8 +1535,35 @@ namespace Ogre
     //---------------------------------------------------------------------
     void SubMesh::destroyShadowMappingVaos()
     {
-        if( mVao[VpNormal].empty() || mVao[VpShadow].empty() || mVao[VpNormal][0] == mVao[VpShadow][0] )
-            mVao[VpShadow].clear();  // Using the same Vaos for both shadow mapping and regular rendering
+        // JAHSHAKA PATCH 0088: THE ALIAS TEST IS PER ENTRY, NOT PER LIST.
+        //
+        // The test this replaced read `mVao[VpNormal][0] == mVao[VpShadow][0]` and
+        // decided for the WHOLE list from it, so only two shapes were legal: every
+        // entry an alias of the normal VAO, or every entry independent. A MIXED
+        // list — which is what a per-LOD-level shadow list wants to be, with an
+        // optimized (shrunk, position-only) VAO at level 0 and the normal VAOs
+        // aliased for the coarse levels — read as fully independent: the aliased
+        // entries were destroyed here and destroyed a second time by ~SubMesh
+        // through mVao[VpNormal], throwing "Vertex Buffer has already been
+        // destroyed or doesn't belong to this VaoManager".
+        //
+        // Erasing every entry that IS an entry of the normal list, and destroying
+        // only what is left, is the same answer for both pure shapes (all-aliased
+        // erases everything, all-independent erases nothing) and the correct one
+        // for a mixed list. The lists are a handful of entries long (one per LOD
+        // level), so the linear search costs nothing measurable and needs no extra
+        // state to keep in sync.
+        {
+            VertexArrayObjectArray::iterator itor = mVao[VpShadow].begin();
+            while( itor != mVao[VpShadow].end() )
+            {
+                if( std::find( mVao[VpNormal].begin(), mVao[VpNormal].end(), *itor ) !=
+                    mVao[VpNormal].end() )
+                    itor = mVao[VpShadow].erase( itor );
+                else
+                    ++itor;
+            }
+        }
 
         destroyVaos( mVao[VpShadow], mParent->mVaoManager );
 
